893:
(a) The fire protection account shall establish specified customer classes that will be charged specific monthly fees based on the amount of resources needed to address fire risk and to suppress fires and emergencies when they occur.
(b) The fire protection utility classes will encompass all developed properties within the city and are defined follows:
(1) The residential class shall consist of all developed residential properties, either single-family or multifamily customers per the applicable definition. Residential classes shall consist of two categories:
a. Residences up to 1,999 square feet of total space; and
b. Residences of 2,000 square feet or more of total space.
(2) The non-residential class shall consist of seven categories:
a. Commercial, industrial, institutional or public properties comprised of less than 800 cumulative square feet of building space;
b. commercial, industrial, institutional or public properties comprised of more than 800 square feet of building space but less than 5,000 cumulative square feet of building space;
c. Commercial, industrial, institutional or public properties comprised of more than 5,000 square feet of building space but less than 19,999 cumulative square feet of building space;
d. Commercial, industrial, institutional or public properties comprised of more than 20,000 square feet of building space;
e. Churches of less than 20,000 square feet of building space;
f. Churches of more than 20,000 square feet of building space;
g. Tier 2 properties regardless of size.
(c) Documentation pertaining to the fire protection account customer classes shall be kept on file in the office of the city clerk for public inspection.
(Ord. of 6-12-2018(1), § 24-37)

903:
It shall be the duty of the fire chief or his designee:
(1) To inspect all buildings and premises not less than once a year;
(2) To inspect all places of public assembly not less than twice per year;
(3) To inspect, not less than four times a year, all hazardous manufacturing processes, storage or installations of gases, chemicals, oils, explosives, and flammable materials;
(4) To inspect as often as he shall find necessary to the enforcement of this chapter all buildings and premises in the city; and
(5) Any business located within the City of Cochran shall/will have an annual fire inspection performed by the Cochran Fire Department. If any violation(s) is found upon the initial inspection, the business will be given a maximum of 30 days to correct the violation(s). If a second inspection is performed and violation(s) is not corrected, the fire department will give the business a written warning and return in 30 days. If a third inspection must be performed and violation(s) is not corrected, the fire department will issue a fine of $50.00 and then return in 30 days. If a fourth inspection must be performed and violation(s) is not corrected, the fire department will issue a fine of $100.00 and have the right to close the business, depending on the severity of the code violation.
(Code 1999, § 3-704; Code 2011, § 24-25; Ord. of 3-9-2021)

936:
